{"id":"10.4.2","doc":"T","title":"RTC States","words":429,"blocks":[{"t":"p","s":[{"n":1,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"ESP32-S3 has three main RTC states: Active, Monitor, and Sleep."}]},{"n":2,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"The transition process among these states can be seen in Figure 10.4-1."}]}]},{"t":"fig","src":"images/trm-fig-10_4-1.png","alt":"Figure 10.4-1. RTC States","caption":"Figure 10.4-1. RTC States"},{"t":"p","s":[{"n":3,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"Under different RTC states, different power domains are powered up or down by default, but can also be force-powered-up (FPU) or force-powered-down (FPD) individually based on actual requirements."}]},{"n":4,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"For details, please refer to Table 10.4-1."}]}]},{"t":"table","caption":"Table 10.4-1. RTC Statues Transition","head":[[{"t":"text","v":"Power Domain Category"}],[{"t":"text","v":"Power Domain Sub-category"}],[{"t":"text","v":"Active"}],[{"t":"text","v":"Monitor"}],[{"t":"text","v":"Sleep"}]],"rows":[{"n":5,"c":[[{"t":"text","v":"RTC (notes 1, 2)"}],[{"t":"text","v":"Power Management Unit (note 3)"}],[{"t":"text","v":"ON"}],[{"t":"text","v":"ON"}],[{"t":"text","v":"ON"}]]},{"n":6,"c":[[{"t":"text","v":"RTC (notes 1, 2)"}],[{"t":"text","v":"RTC Peripherals (note 4)"}],[{"t":"text","v":"ON"}],[{"t":"text","v":"ON"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF"}]]},{"n":7,"c":[[{"t":"text","v":"Digital"}],[{"t":"text","v":"CPU (note 5)"}],[{"t":"text","v":"ON"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF*"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF*"}]]},{"n":8,"c":[[{"t":"text","v":"Digital"}],[{"t":"text","v":"Wireless digital circuits (note 6)"}],[{"t":"text","v":"ON"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF*"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF*"}]]},{"n":9,"c":[[{"t":"text","v":"Digital"}],[{"t":"text","v":"Digital Core (note 7)"}],[{"t":"text","v":"ON"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF*"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF"}]]},{"n":10,"c":[[{"t":"text","v":"Digital"}],[{"t":"text","v":"PD Peripherals"}],[{"t":"text","v":"ON"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF*"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF*"}]]},{"n":11,"c":[[{"t":"text","v":"Analog"}],[{"t":"field","v":"RC_FAST_CLK"}],[{"t":"text","v":"ON"}],[{"t":"text","v":"ON"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF"}]]},{"n":12,"c":[[{"t":"text","v":"Analog"}],[{"t":"field","v":"XTAL_CLK"}],[{"t":"text","v":"ON"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF"}]]},{"n":13,"c":[[{"t":"text","v":"Analog"}],[{"t":"text","v":"PLL"}],[{"t":"text","v":"ON"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF"}],[{"t":"text","v":"OFF"}]]},{"n":14,"c":[[{"t":"text","v":"Analog"}],[{"t":"text","v":"RF Circuits"}],[{"t":"text","v":"-"}],[{"t":"text","v":"-"}],[{"t":"text","v":"-"}]]}]},{"t":"p","s":[{"n":15,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"* Configurable."}]}]},{"t":"list","ordered":true,"items":[[{"t":"p","s":[{"n":16,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"RTC slow memory supports "},{"t":"num","v":"8 KB","k":"size"},{"t":"text","v":" SRAM, which can be used to reserve memory or to store "},{"t":"block","v":"ULP","id":"ulp"},{"t":"text","v":" instructions and/or data."}]},{"n":17,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"This memory (starting address is 0x5000_0000) can be accessed by CPU via PIF bus, and should be force-power-on."}]},{"n":18,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"RTC slow memory is always OFF under the RTC state of Monitor with only one exception when the ULP coprocessor is working."}]}]}],[{"t":"p","s":[{"n":19,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"RTC fast memory supports "},{"t":"num","v":"8 KB","k":"size"},{"t":"text","v":" SRAM, which can be used to reserve memory."}]},{"n":20,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"This memory can be accessed by CPU via IRAM0/DRAM0, and should be forced-power-up."}]}]}],[{"t":"p","s":[{"n":21,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"ESP32-S3's power management unit is specially designed to be \"always-on\", which means it is always on when the chip is powered up."}]},{"n":22,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"Therefore, users cannot FPU or FPD the power management unit."}],"warn":true}]}],[{"t":"p","s":[{"n":23,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"The RTC peripherals include "},{"t":"xref","id":"2","c":[{"t":"text","v":"Chapter 2"}]},{"t":"text","v":" "},{"t":"i","c":[{"t":"text","v":"ULP Coprocessor (ULP-FSM, ULP-RISC-V)"}]},{"t":"text","v":" and "},{"t":"xref","id":"39","c":[{"t":"text","v":"Chapter 39"}]},{"t":"text","v":" "},{"t":"i","c":[{"t":"text","v":"On-Chip Sensors and Analog Signal Processing"}]},{"t":"text","v":" (i.e., "},{"t":"block","v":"temperature sensor","id":"temp-sensor"},{"t":"text","v":" controller and "},{"t":"block","v":"SAR ADC","id":"adc"},{"t":"text","v":" controller)."}]}]}],[{"t":"p","s":[{"n":24,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"CPU can be powered down separately in light-sleep, but retention DMA is required to resume CPU."}]}]}],[{"t":"p","s":[{"n":25,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"Power domain Wireless digital circuits includes "},{"t":"block","v":"Wi-Fi","id":"wifi"},{"t":"text","v":" MAC, BT and BB (Base Band)."}]}]}],[{"t":"p","s":[{"n":26,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"When the digital core of the digital is powered down, all components in the digital are turned off."}]},{"n":27,"c":[{"t":"text","v":"It's worth noting that, ESP32-S3's ROM and SRAM are no longer controlled as independent power domains, thus cannot be force-powered-up or force-powered-down when the digital core is powered down."}],"warn":true}]}]]}]}